; Hook the game to the 128K music player (128K machines only)
;
; Called by #R$6000 when the machine is not a 48K. It turns three one-instruction stubs of the 48K code into CALLs
; (store $CD, the opcode of CALL, over the first byte): #R$860C (start of the game: the call enters the music bank
; through #R$85F3), $88D5 in #R$887B (end of the ending sequence, through #R$85E8) and $A31E in the key-release wait
; (through #R$85EE). Each stub is a 3-byte instruction whose operand already is the address of the 128K entry routine,
; and each of those pages in the music bank and calls the player there with #R$85C2.
; .
; When L is nonzero (the loader found an AY chip) it also turns the first instruction of the beeper sound routine #R$871A into JP
; #R$85D5 ($C3 stored), which sends sound effects to the AY player instead of the beeper.
;
; L 1 if the loader found an AY chip, 0 if not (the beeper effects stay)
@label=Patch128KHooks
@isub=LD A,OP_CALL
c$605F LD A,$CD             ; opcode of CALL
 $6061 LD ($860C),A         ; the instruction at $860C (LD HL,$85F3) becomes CALL $85F3
 $6064 LD ($88D5),A         ; $88D5 (LD HL,$85E8) becomes CALL $85E8
 $6067 LD ($A31E),A         ; $A31E (a RET followed by the operand bytes of CALL $85EE) becomes CALL $85EE
 $606A LD A,L        ;
 $606B OR A          ;
 $606C RET Z         ;
@isub=LD A,OP_JP
 $606D LD A,$C3             ; opcode of JP
 $606F LD ($871A),A         ; $871A (LD HL,$85D5) becomes JP $85D5
 $6072 RET           ;
